WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.980
Kubernetes probes influence whether traffic reaches an application and whether the application is restarted.

2
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:11.960
That makes probes a security and reliability control, not just a deployment checkbox.

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:18.940
Poorly designed probes can hide failures, create noisy restarts, or send traffic before the app is ready.

4
00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.900
Readiness should indicate whether the app is ready to receive requests.

5
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:38.680
An ASP.NET Core app may be alive as a process but not ready because configuration is missing, warm-up is incomplete, migrations are in progress, a required dependency is unavailable, or the app is shutting down and draining requests.

6
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.760
Liveness should indicate whether restart is appropriate.

7
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:46.660
It should not fail only because a downstream dependency is slow for a short period.

8
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:54.440
Restarting healthy application processes during a dependency outage can make recovery harder, increase load, and hide the original signal.

9
00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:00.480
Startup probes can help slow-starting apps avoid premature liveness failures.

10
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:09.620
They are useful when an application has predictable initialization work, cold-start behavior, or dependency checks that take longer than normal request handling.

11
00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:18.700
Health endpoints should avoid exposing secrets, connection strings, raw dependency errors, internal hostnames, or sensitive operational details.

12
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:29.680
Probe timing, thresholds, graceful shutdown, request draining, cancellation, and logging should be reviewed together so availability controls support safe recovery.
